notes               = "Untitled in source. The dedication با یاد فیروزه صابری (ممیز) ('in memory of Firouzeh Saberi (Momayez)') is printed in the outer margin. THE DEDICATEE'S NAME IS THE POEM'S LAST IMAGE: فیروزه is Persian for turquoise, and the mirror in line 8 turns فیروزه‌ای — turquoise — as its final colour, so the elegy spells her name in the glass. The same device as mother-tenderness (184-185), where the nurse's name عاطفه is also the word for tenderness. WORD FLAG, line 2: printed as what reads فابوس, which is not a word; almost certainly فانوس, lantern — one dot's difference, and 'hang the lantern of the moon in the mirror' is the only sense the line will carry. NOT emended in the Persian here, which is kept as printed, but the machine and lantern layers both read it as lantern. چهل تکه is a forty-piece patchwork quilt; کجاوهٔ لیلی is Layla's camel-litter, from Layli and Majnun. 23 Bahman = 11 February 1997. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

برخیزم
فابوس ماه را
در آینه بیاویزم
و آنقدر نام تو را
در پرده‌های اشک بخوانم
تا سطح آینه
چهل تکه‌ای
از... آه... و... آبی... و... فیروزه‌ای شود...
آنگاه...
تو... از زمین سفر کرده...
آن صبح سرد آفتابی...
در حسرت هزار نگاه...
سرد و سپید و پاک
در دست‌های باد
در پله‌های خاک
شاید به راه بیایی
در پرده... با کجاوهٔ لیلی
از راه ماه بیایی...
تهران ۱۳۷۵/۱۱/۲۳

===machine===

Let me rise
and hang the lantern of the moon
in the mirror,
and say your name so many times
through the curtains of tears
that the mirror's surface
turns a forty-piece patchwork
of... sighs... and... blue... and... turquoise...
And then...
you... who have travelled out of the earth...
that cold sunlit morning...
in the longing of a thousand gazes...
cold and white and pure,
in the hands of the wind,
on the stairs of dust —
perhaps you will come along the road,
behind the curtain... with Layla's litter,
and come by way of the moon...
Tehran 1375/11/23

===lantern===

Let me rise
and hang the lantern of the moon
in the mirror,
and say your name so many times
through the curtains of tears
that the mirror's surface
turns a forty-piece patchwork
of sighs, and blue, and turquoise.
And then —
you, who have travelled out of the earth
that cold sunlit morning,
missed by a thousand looks,
cold and white and pure,
in the hands of the wind,
on the stairs of dust —
perhaps you will come along the road,
behind the curtain, with Layla's litter,
and come by way of the moon...

Tehran 1375/11/23

===translation===

===footnotes===

فیروزه is a woman's name and also the word for turquoise. The poem says the name over and
over into a mirror until the glass changes colour, and the colour it arrives at is her name. An
elegy that ends by spelling the dead woman out of the thing it is looking into.

Line 2 is printed as فابوس, which is not a Persian word. It is almost certainly فانوس — *lantern* —
a single dot away, and the line makes sense no other way. The printed reading is kept in the Persian
above; the English takes the lantern.
